A||||2||Brandon86SE||Running without Sending Units, is it bad?||||5||
Z||000000||Brandon86SE||11-24-2001||05:27 PM||||I've put mechanical gauges into my car, 'cause my water temp/oil pressure/ voltmeter never worked properly since buying the car, and I liked the look, and the responsiveness of the mechanical gauges.  (Plus the fact they won't break as easily).  So naturally, I have no electrical Oil Pressure, or Water Temp sending units.  I've noticed nothing wrong with the car since disconnecting everything, but was curious about the topic since the ECM now knows neither value.  What do the rest of you think about this?<P>Brandon<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Field_Front_(sig)_1.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||87GTZ34||11-24-2001||05:37 PM||||There is an ECM temp sensor which is needed.  I believe it is on the front of the intake manifold and it is used to adjust the A/F ratio as the car warms up.  Also, there is a cold start switch in the front of the intake manifold. If you live up north, you'll need that as well. The two you can ignore are the temp sensor in the corner of the head and oil pressure sender.  The oil pressure sensor is also used as a backup to the fuel pump relay so you can still drive your car so keep that sensor hooked up at the engine. GL||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-25-2001||03:14 PM||||The dash gauges/lights and ECM don't share any sensors. If you removed an ECM sensor you'd know because the ECM would now be throwing codes and the car would likely run like crap.<P>When you eliminated the OP sender, you eliminated it's fuel pump switch. How good or bad that is is hard to say. At the very least, I'd recomend you go buy a spare fuel pump relay to keep in the car. Other wise if the relay quits you'll now need a tow truck.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
